Original commit body from 0f9257c07ccfb2ccfc59c4e3c6771db828b48316:

Every tree-sitter parse that needed error recovery was analysed as if
complete: tree.root_node.has_error was on the tree and nothing read it, so a
file whose parse died looked the same as a file that declares nothing.

record_partial_parse(run, path, tree) in analyze/base.py lists such a file in
limits.failed_files with a "partial_parse:" reason and a count of damaged
nodes (ERROR, MISSING, or flagged with no flagged child; tree-sitter-markdown
flags pipe-table cells with no ERROR node at all). It is a disclosure, never a
gate: the file is still analysed and nothing is dropped, so the fortran trap
(a lagging grammar flags valid code) cannot remove declarations.

Wired into the shared TreeSitterAnalyzer loop (and every analyzer delegating
to it) and the go, java, js_ts, kotlin, c, objc, csharp, php, ruby and lua
loops. objc records after WI-lafom's rewrite retry, so only unrepaired damage
is reported. On crun 74 files, AFNetworking 34, phoenix 114, hummingbird 7,
alertmanager 0; before, none on any.

Residuals filed: WI-tikul (26 custom loops not wired), WI-somod (every .h is
parsed by both C and ObjC, half the .h rows are cross-dialect), WI-vufur (a
verify-claims caveat only where it discriminates).

